  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 103 PM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nThe wetter weather pattern will continue on through this weekend. \nFor today, there is a risk for severe weather and flash flooding. \nThe eastern plains should be located near the right entrance region \nof a jet over the central US with a small disturbance expected in \nthe flow aloft. GOES-19 satellite imagery shows a cirrus shield \ncurrently over the mountains that could initially inhibit convective \ndevelopment. Nevertheless, convective development is starting to \noccur across the foothills and in Larimer county. We expect showers \nand thunderstorms to develop across the foothills early this \nafternoon and move into the I-25 corridor by mid-to-late afternoon. \nThese storms will then move off to the east in the late afternoon \nand early evening. \n\nThe previously mentioned cirrus shield could hinder rain coverage \nand/or push this timing a bit later. The biggest threat for today \nwill be heavy rainfall with some episodes of flash flooding possible \nacross the area. The Weather Prediction Center highlights the \nfoothills, I-25 corridor, and the eastern plains in a slight (2/4) \nrisk for excessive rainfall for today. Several ingredients are in \nplace for this threat. Thus, a Flood Watch is in effect from 2pm to \n10pm this afternoon and evening. See the hydro discussion for more \ndetails. \n\nThe Storm Prediction Center highlights the plains in a \nslight (2/5) risk. MLCAPE values are forecast to be around 1000-1200 \nJ/kg and 0-6 km shear will be around 30-40 kts. Large hail and \ndamaging winds are possible from storms today, with the highest \nchances along the Palmer Divide.\n\nTomorrow will bring similar threats as today, though we expect less \ncoverage of thunderstorms. Thus, the threats will be more localized \nin nature. MLCAPE and bulk shear values are forecast to be lower \nthan today. The highest chances for showers and thunderstorms \ntomorrow should stay along/near the Palmer Divide, but there will\nstill be scattered storms possible for most of our plains.\n\nHigh pressure will shift closer to our area for Monday and Tuesday \nfurther lowering rain chances and raising temperatures to around \n90 degrees for the plains. By Wednesday, high pressure should \nshift off to the southwest, opening the door for a disturbance to \nmove through the flow aloft and bringing back some isolated to\nscattered rain chances. This will only be a brief break as high \npressure will start to build over the Four Corners region. \nTemperatures are expected to return to the low to mid 90s across \nthe plains for the end of the week and into next weekend.",
